        <div id="is-protein-powder-low-fodmap" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Is Protein Powder Low FODMAP?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Protein is not a FODMAP. FODMAPs are fermentable carbohydrates: fermentable oligosaccharides, disaccharides, monosaccharides and polyols. In practice that means fructans and galacto-oligosaccharides, lactose, excess fructose, and sugar alcohols such as sorbitol and mannitol. A pure protein with no carbohydrate attached, such as egg white or a highly filtered whey isolate, has nothing for gut bacteria to ferment in the way FODMAPs are fermented. So the protein itself is rarely the problem. The problem is the carbohydrate that comes with it, either naturally or added.
          </p>

          <p>
            That gives two routes to a low FODMAP protein powder. The first is a certified product, which has been lab-tested by Monash University or FODMAP Friendly and is low FODMAP at the stated serving. The second is a powder whose ingredients leave no room for FODMAPs: whey isolate, egg white or collagen with no added fibers, sugar alcohols, honey or fruit powders. The first route is more certain. The second is cheaper and usually fine, but it relies on your reading of the label rather than a lab result.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Who this is for</h3>

          <p>
            The low FODMAP diet was developed at Monash University for irritable bowel syndrome, and it has good evidence for reducing IBS symptoms in many patients. It is designed in three phases: a strict elimination phase of typically two to six weeks, a structured reintroduction to find which FODMAP groups you react to, and a long-term personalized diet that is as broad as you can tolerate. Staying on the strict phase indefinitely is not the goal and can reduce the diversity of your gut bacteria.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you have gut symptoms but no diagnosis, see a doctor first. Symptoms that look like IBS can come from celiac disease, inflammatory bowel disease and other conditions that need different treatment, and red flags such as weight loss, blood in the stool or night-time symptoms need prompt assessment. A registered dietitian trained in the low FODMAP approach is the best guide through the phases. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-sensitive-stomach/">protein powder for a sensitive stomach</a> guide covers gut-friendly choices more broadly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="isolate-vs-concentrate" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Whey Isolate vs Concentrate: How Much Lactose Is in Each?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Lactose is the FODMAP most protein powder buyers are actually worried about, and the difference between whey types is large. Whey protein concentrate keeps more of the milk's carbohydrate, and its lactose content varies widely by product, often a few percent of the powder by weight, which can add up to a gram or more per scoop. Whey protein isolate is filtered further to around 90 percent protein, and most of the lactose goes with the filtered-out fraction, typically leaving well under a gram per scoop.
          </p>

          <p>
            Monash treats lactose as low FODMAP at small amounts, roughly 1 g per serving or less, because most people with lactose malabsorption tolerate that much. That puts a typical whey isolate comfortably on the low side and many concentrates near or over the line. Naked Whey Isolate states about 0.1 g of lactose per serving; Isopure Unflavored lists 0 g of carbohydrate per scoop, which caps lactose below the rounding threshold. Those are the numbers to look for.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
