            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Two caveats. First, isolate is a protein claim, not a guarantee: many popular powders labeled as whey are blends of isolate and concentrate, and the order of ingredients tells you which dominates. Read the ingredient list, not just the front of the tub. Our <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate</a> comparison explains the difference, and our <a href="/best-lactose-free-protein-powder/">best lactose-free protein powder</a> guide goes deeper on lactose specifically.
          </p>

          <p>
            Second, a whey isolate with a clean protein base can still be high FODMAP because of what is added to it. A lactose-free isolate sweetened with sorbitol, or blended with inulin for fiber, fails the test. That is the subject of the next section.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="high-fodmap-ingredients" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Ingredients That Make a Protein Powder High FODMAP
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most protein powders that cause IBS-type symptoms do so because of an added ingredient, not the protein. These are the ones to look for on the label. Some are obvious; others hide behind gut-health or low-sugar marketing.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Ingredient</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">FODMAP group</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Where it appears</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Inulin, chicory root fiber</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fructans (oligosaccharides)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High-fiber and gut-health powders</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">FOS, fructooligosaccharides</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fructans</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Prebiotic blends</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sorbitol, mannitol, xylitol, maltitol, isomalt</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Polyols</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sugar-free and low-carb powders</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Honey, agave, fruit powders (apple, mango, pear)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Excess fructose; sorbitol</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Natural and superfood blends</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Whey concentrate, milk powder</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lactose</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Budget wheys and creamy blends</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pea protein concentrate, soy flour</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">GOS (oligosaccharides)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cheaper plant blends</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Inulin and chicory root</h3>

          <p>
            Inulin is a fructan, usually extracted from chicory root, and it is the single most common reason a protein powder marketed for gut health sets off IBS symptoms. Brands add it as a prebiotic fiber or to raise the fiber count. Fructans are among the FODMAPs people with IBS most commonly react to. If a label lists inulin, chicory root, chicory root fiber or FOS, it is not a low FODMAP product. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-with-fiber/">protein powder with fiber</a> guide lists several such products, and some of them are excellent for people who tolerate fructans.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sugar alcohols</h3>

          <p>
            Sorbitol, mannitol, xylitol, maltitol and isomalt are polyols, poorly absorbed in the small intestine, and both osmotic and fermentable in the large intestine. They appear in sugar-free powders and many protein bars. Erythritol is a sugar alcohol too but behaves differently: most of it is absorbed and excreted unchanged, and Monash has not classed it with the high FODMAP polyols, although large amounts still cause symptoms in some people. If you are in a strict elimination phase, it is simplest to avoid all of them, and our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-artificial-sweeteners/">protein powder without artificial sweeteners</a> guide covers the sweetener question more broadly.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gums and thickeners</h3>

          <p>
            Small amounts of xanthan gum and guar gum are generally tolerated, and partially hydrolyzed guar gum is sometimes used as a gentler fiber in IBS. Acacia gum and large amounts of mixed gums bother some people. Gums are not a reason to reject a powder outright, but if a short-ingredient powder works and a heavily thickened one does not, the gums are a likely culprit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="certification" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Monash vs FODMAP Friendly Certification: What They Mean
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two programs certify low FODMAP products. Monash University, whose researchers developed the diet, runs the Monash University Low FODMAP Certified program, with its own logo and a product listing in the Monash FODMAP app. FODMAP Friendly, an Australian program, runs its own certification with a separate logo and app. Both lab-test the finished product and certify it as low FODMAP at a stated serving size. Neither is a marketing claim the brand can award itself.
          </p>

          <p>
            Three details matter for protein powder. First, certification applies to a specific product and formula, so a brand's chocolate can be certified while its vanilla is not. Second, it applies at the stated serving: two scoops of a powder certified at one scoop may not be low FODMAP, because FODMAPs add up. Third, certification is reviewed over time, and products can drop out of the program, so check the app rather than an old listing image.
          </p>

          <p>
            The words low FODMAP or gut-friendly on a label without one of the logos are not certification. They may be accurate, and a carefully formulated powder can be low FODMAP without paying for certification, but you are relying on the brand's own judgment. That is fine for a whey isolate with two ingredients. It is less fine for a complex plant blend with fibers and flavors, where the FODMAP content is hard to predict from the label.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE PRACTICAL RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Certified for the elimination phase, simple labels for the long run.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">During strict elimination, a certified powder at its certified serving removes guesswork. Once reintroduction shows what you tolerate, an uncertified isolate or egg white with a short label is usually just as good and costs far less.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="plant-egg-collagen" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Pea, Rice, Egg and Collagen on a Low FODMAP Diet
          </h2>

          <p>
            Low FODMAP dieters who also avoid dairy have fewer certain options, because plant proteins carry some of the oligosaccharides that make whole legumes a problem. How much remains in the powder depends on how it is processed, and testing so far has not given a single answer for each protein type.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pea protein</h3>

          <p>
            Monash previously listed pea protein as low FODMAP at a small serving, but later testing of multiple brands found results that varied, so pea protein powders are uncertain unless the finished product is certified. Pea protein isolates generally have more of the oligosaccharides removed than concentrates, but the label rarely tells you enough to know. If you need a pea-based powder, a certified product such as Better Blends is the safer choice. Our <a href="/best-pea-protein-powder/">pea protein powder</a> guide covers the category on nutrition.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rice protein</h3>

          <p>
            Sprouted brown rice protein is listed as low FODMAP at a tested serving in the Monash app, which makes it the most reliable single plant protein for this diet. The trade-offs are taste, a lower leucine content than whey and the inorganic arsenic that rice can carry. Rotate it with other sources rather than relying on it every day; our <a href="/best-brown-rice-protein-powder/">brown rice protein powder</a> guide covers the testing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Egg white and collagen</h3>

          <p>
            Egg white and collagen peptides contain essentially no carbohydrate in their unflavored form, so they are low FODMAP by nature. Egg white is a complete protein; collagen is not, lacking tryptophan and being low in leucine, so count it toward total protein but do not rely on it as your main shake. Our <a href="/best-collagen-protein-powder/">best collagen protein powder</a> guide covers what collagen is good for. Flavored versions of both can add FODMAPs, so the unflavored versions are the safe default.
          </p>

          <p>
            Soy protein isolate is another possibility, since soy milk made from soy protein tests low FODMAP while soy milk from whole beans does not, but soy protein powders have not been tested widely. Our sibling guides to the <a href="/best-soy-free-protein-powder/">best soy-free protein powder</a> and <a href="/best-dairy-free-protein-powder/">best dairy-free protein powder</a> cover those other restrictions.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-on-amazon" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Buying Low FODMAP Protein Powder on Amazon: Logos, Labels and Servings
          </h2>

          <p>
            Low FODMAP is a popular keyword on Amazon, and listing titles use it freely. Some products carrying it are certified, some are carefully formulated but uncertified, and some simply contain whey isolate and hope for the best while adding inulin or a sugar alcohol. The title cannot tell you which. Here is how to check before ordering.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Look for the logo, then check the app</h3>

          <p>
            Scroll through the listing images for a Monash or FODMAP Friendly logo on the actual product packaging, not a graphic made for the listing. Then search the product in the Monash FODMAP app or the FODMAP Friendly app. If it is there, note the certified serving size. If a listing says certified but neither app shows the product, treat the claim as unconfirmed and contact the brand.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Read the ingredient panel image</h3>

          <p>
            Find the label image with the full ingredient list and scan it for inulin, chicory root, FOS, sorbitol, mannitol, xylitol, maltitol, honey, agave, fruit powders and whey concentrate. Bullet points and A+ content are marketing; the panel is the evidence. Amazon images can lag reformulations, so read the tub again when it arrives, before your first scoop.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Buy from the brand or Amazon, and note the lot</h3>

          <p>
            Check the Ships from and Sold by lines. Specialist low FODMAP brands often sell through their own Amazon storefronts, and buying there or from Amazon directly cuts the risk of counterfeit or old stock with an earlier formula. Note the lot number and best-by date: if a powder you tolerated suddenly causes symptoms, a formula change or a different lot is a likelier explanation than your gut changing, and the brand needs the lot to check.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Subscribe & Save, sizes and cost per 25 g</h3>

          <p>
            Certified powders cost more and come in small tubs, so the size and flavor selector matters: the price at the top of a listing may belong to a smaller bag, a sample pack or a different flavor. Subscribe & Save typically takes 5 percent off, and up to 15 percent with five or more subscriptions in a month, but do not subscribe during an elimination phase, when you may switch after a few weeks. Compare on cost per 25 g of protein by dividing price by servings, then by protein per serving, and multiplying by 25. Our <a href="/tools/protein-cost-calculator/">protein cost calculator</a> does the math, and our <a href="/amazon-protein-powder/">Amazon protein powder</a> guide covers the platform's wider quirks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We started with protein powders genuinely sold on Amazon US. We ranked certified products first, reporting certification exactly as the brand states it, then uncertified powders whose ingredient lists contain no inulin, chicory, FOS, polyols, honey, fruit powders or whey concentrate at the time of writing. Within each group we weighed protein per serving, ingredient simplicity, sport certifications and cost per 25 g of protein. We did not lab-test FODMAP content or run taste panels; see <a href="/how-we-test/">how we test</a> for our method.
          </p>

          <p>
            We did not include pea-only powders without certification, because published testing on pea protein FODMAP content is inconsistent, and we left out Garden of Life Sport and Huel Black Edition on the basis of the heavy-metal data in our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">protein powder without heavy metals</a> guide. Nothing here is medical advice. If you have IBS or another digestive condition, a registered dietitian trained in the low FODMAP approach should guide the diet, and our guide to <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein you need</a> can help you decide whether you need a powder at all.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a gut-health powder with inulin</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Inulin and chicory root fiber are fructans, one of the FODMAP groups people with IBS most often react to, yet they appear in many powders sold for gut health. If the label lists inulin, chicory root or FOS, the powder is not low FODMAP, whatever the title says.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Doubling a certified serving</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Certification applies at the stated serving size. A powder certified as low FODMAP at one scoop may not be low FODMAP at two, because FODMAPs add up across a serving and across a meal. Stick to the certified serving during elimination, then test larger amounts later.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming any whey is low lactose</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Whey concentrate can carry a gram or more of lactose per scoop, and many tubs labeled whey are isolate and concentrate blends. Read the ingredient order and look for a stated lactose or carbohydrate figure. An isolate at 0 g carbohydrate is the safe default.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating sugar-free as FODMAP-free</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sugar-free powders often use sorbitol, maltitol, xylitol or mannitol, all polyols that are poorly absorbed and commonly trigger IBS symptoms. Erythritol behaves differently but can still bother some people in large amounts. Check the sweetener, not just the sugar line.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Staying in the elimination phase indefinitely</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The low FODMAP diet is meant to be short-term, followed by structured reintroduction to find your actual triggers. Staying strict for months narrows your diet and may reduce gut bacterial diversity. Work with a registered dietitian to move through the phases.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the best low FODMAP protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For a strict elimination phase, a certified powder: Casa de Sante Advanced Whey, which its listing says is FODMAP Friendly certified, or Better Blends Vanilla Dream, which the brand says is Monash certified and is dairy-free. For everyday value, an unflavored whey isolate with no fibers or sugar alcohols, such as Isopure Zero Carb Unflavored, works well.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is whey protein low FODMAP?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Whey isolate is generally low FODMAP because filtering removes most of the lactose, typically leaving well under a gram per scoop, which is around Monash's low FODMAP threshold. Whey concentrate carries more lactose and varies by product. Either can become high FODMAP if the brand adds inulin, sugar alcohols or fruit powders.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is pea protein low FODMAP?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It is uncertain. Monash once listed pea protein as low FODMAP at a small serving, but later testing found results varied between brands. Pea protein isolate generally retains fewer oligosaccharides than concentrate. During elimination, choose a certified pea product such as Better Blends, or use egg white or brown rice protein instead.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What ingredients should I avoid in protein powder for IBS?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  On a low FODMAP diet, avoid inulin, chicory root, FOS, the polyols sorbitol, mannitol, xylitol, maltitol and isomalt, honey, agave, apple, pear and mango powders, and whey concentrate or milk powder. Large amounts of mixed gums also bother some people. Unflavored isolates and egg white avoid nearly all of these.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the difference between Monash and FODMAP Friendly certification?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Both lab-test finished products and certify them as low FODMAP at a stated serving. Monash University developed the diet and runs its program through the Monash FODMAP app; FODMAP Friendly is an Australian program with its own logo and app. Either logo means the product was tested, unlike an unverified low FODMAP claim.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is collagen low FODMAP?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Unflavored collagen peptides contain essentially no carbohydrate, so they are low FODMAP by nature. Flavored collagen can add fruit powders or sweeteners, so check the label. Collagen is an incomplete protein, low in leucine and lacking tryptophan, so count it toward daily protein but use whey isolate, egg white or a certified blend for main shakes.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can protein powder cause IBS symptoms?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes, though usually because of additives rather than the protein. Inulin, sugar alcohols, lactose in whey concentrate and large serving sizes are common triggers. If a powder causes bloating, pain or diarrhea, try an unflavored isolate or certified product at a modest serving, and see a doctor or dietitian if symptoms persist.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
